-- title:
--   Z(K)N(A_K) is closed in GL₂(A_K)
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a number field, i.e. a field $K$ carrying the `NumberField` structure, and let $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$ denote `AdelicGL2 (𝓞 K) K`, the group of invertible $2\times 2$ matrices over the adele ring of $K$ relative to its ring of integers, with its natural topology. Consider two subgroups of this group: `rationalCentre K`, the range of the monoid homomorphism obtained by composing the embedding of $K^\times$ into $\mathrm{GL}_2(K)$ as scalar matrices, via $a \mapsto a \cdot 1$ (the units map of the ring homomorphism `Matrix.scalar (Fin 2)`), with the homomorphism `globalPoints (𝓞 K) K` from $\mathrm{GL}_2(K)$ to $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$; and `adelicUnipotent K`, the range of `unipotentGL2Hom` over the adele ring, i.e. the group of upper unipotent matrices $n(x)$ with $x \in \mathbb{A}_K$. The theorem asserts that the join `rationalCentreUnipotent K` of these two subgroups, that is the subgroup $Z(K)N(\mathbb{A}_K)$ generated by the rational central scalars together with all adelic upper unipotent matrices, is a closed subset of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$.
--
--   Closedness of $Z(K)N(\mathbb{A}_K)$ is the topological hypothesis under which the quotient measure on $Z(K)N(\mathbb{A}_K)\backslash\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{A}_K)$ and the associated quotient-integral formulae are available; it rests on the discreteness of $K$ in $\mathbb{A}_K$, used here through the finiteness of the set of elements of $K$ whose adelic image lies in a given compact set. It is invoked in the Rankin–Selberg computations and in the comparisons of integrals over the unipotent, rational-torus and rational-centre quotients.
